# We Are the Vanguard—Come, Let Us Advance!
Tamil title: முன்னணிப் படையினர் நாம் முன்னேறிச் செல்வோம், வாரீர்!

You are reviled as innocents, herds of sheep, people who have lost their senses, strayed from the path, lacked reason and been deceived—all because you joined me, dared to take a separate path, came forward to reveal the pain caused by the leader's words and the shame caused by his deeds, refused to continue working under his leadership, and came forward to establish the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam.

The very leader who once praised you as brave, resolute, self-sacrificing and wise now abuses you as innocents, a flock of sheep, fools and people who stumbled. This may have caused you anger and sorrow. I recognise my duty to console you, and therefore send in this letter the thoughts surging through my mind.

Periyar and those with him sing abuse that wounds your hearts, accusing you because you joined me. I regret it. Truth sometimes suffers injury, honesty faces crisis, purity is scorned, virtue blamed and slander spread. These are among the perverse events in this strange world. You are now being abused in that fashion. Periyar speaks degradingly of you, and the mind naturally suffers.

At precisely such times the heart must gain greater resolve, fill with endurance and lose all panic. Like a vessel travelling without fear of waves, we must cultivate the quality of disregarding abuse and engaging in the work we have undertaken.

He will call you innocents and a herd of sheep. Dismiss these as meaningless words. Understand that the abuse is the result of his disappointment: he is distressed and therefore reviles you. Once you recognise this, I assure you that whenever he abuses you, you will feel like laughing rather than becoming angry. That is how I laugh.

You may ask: “That may suit you, but did he call you innocent or a sheep? Is he not abusing us, who labour for a principle?” Friends, I too am the target of his abuse and condemnation. Harsher words than those hurled at you are thrown at me and the comrades working with me:

Traitors! Judases! Devadattas! People seeking a livelihood through public life! Belly-fillers! Self-seekers! Cheats!

Such “ritual offerings” are endless and daily. That is not all. Stories say ancient ascetics pronounced curses; likewise, the father who gave us rationalism pronounces many curses:

They will be driven away! They will flee in terror! They will disappear! They will join another party! They will contest elections! They will wither away!

Because I understand this abuse and these curses as products of his disappointment, they bring me laughter rather than anger.

It is certainly a disappointment to him. In public life he has faced and defeated opposition from many people many times. He thought this too was opposition of that kind. It is not. It is not opposition at all, but the effort of wounded hearts to find another path. As he comes to understand this, anger rises within him. He imagines he can find peace only by calling you innocents and me a traitor, and so he abuses us.

Should one who must offer the nation sound and virtuous counsel, praise rationalism and fight social degradation occupy himself with insulting us? It resembles one who should demolish forts and bastions shouting before a palm-leaf hut, or a lion pouncing upon mice. Should a person capable of such great achievements shoot arrows of abuse at these “unfortunates” who shed tears? I grieve, wondering why this decline occurred. If you analyse the situation, you too will reach the same conclusion.

Resolve and analytical ability have flourished within you. That is why you disregard his word “innocents” and show enthusiasm for constructive work. I rejoice, friends, thank you and praise your character.

Many branches of the Dravida Munnetra Kazhagam have already appeared properly and enthusiastically. You used three methods: a Dravidar Kazhagam branch gathered and changed its name; a branch gathered, dissolved itself and created a new organisation; or Dravidian comrades gathered and founded a new organisation.

No report has come from anywhere that a branch debated whether to rename or dissolve itself and a majority resolved that it must remain the Dravidar Kazhagam. News continues to arrive that the DMK was formed through one of the three methods. What greater victory does honesty require?

What can a thousand words of condemnation hurled at us do? Does this news of victory not swiftly heal our wounded minds? As he sees it, his abuse will continue increasing. Do not grieve. Hardship naturally occurs while doing one's duty; even when picking a rose, a thorn pricks. The first reward for a good undertaking begun by pure hearts is abuse. Let us endure it.

He now calls you innocents and me a traitor. Yet he once called you “gems” and said of me, “I am giving him the key to the cash box.” On a thousand platforms he said: “Instead of travelling with me after earning an M.A., would this Annadurai not already be a minister had he joined Congress? What profit does he gain by remaining with me?”

Many times he praised you as comrades who followed him despite the abuse of townspeople and hostility of family and relatives. Today he denies that you and I possess minds of our own. In anger he says all manner of things. What loss does that cause us? We have received abundant praise from him; let us remember it and find consolation.

They call me traitor and Judas and even display fragments of history. What benefit they gain I do not know, but I am certain I suffer no loss. If they call me a traitor today, can I be frightened away or the public made to hate me?

I am abused because I differed with him over his marriage. Can abuse make me abandon what I wholeheartedly believe? He did not train me to do that, and ought to know it. Bewilderment has made him forget.

When he was abused by the country's “great” party and by most ordinary and learned people as a traitor to religion and nation and an enemy of Brahma, I became his “servant,” despite being an educated graduate.

Hearing him called many kinds of traitor did not make me wonder whether I should work with such a man. I regarded sharing that abuse as my good fortune.

Some “supporters” now wring their hands saying they raised me and gave me influence. Let them refrain from raising anyone hereafter. But let them understand one thing: working with Periyar brought me not only the love and support of Kazhagam comrades, but an equal measure of abuse, curses, opposition and ridicule from outsiders. Other parties gave me the same title of traitor they gave him. I shared the abuse of other parties with him. Now some Kazhagam comrades abuse me too. That is all.

The ability to endure abuse from other parties while working with Periyar has not withered; it has grown through hardship and his lessons. Whether he calls me traitor in anger, or others do so believing abuse of me will please him, I console myself by remembering that dominant powers, political gamblers and Vedic perversions similarly call Periyar—a man labouring for honesty, justice and Dravidian rights—a traitor to nation and religion. Both accusations arise from anger born of disappointment.

I do not wish to use the DMK for political gambling. Had I wanted such gambling, I would have tried to use the organisation in which I worked for years. Only if that proved impossible would I need a new attempt. Did I ever make such an attempt and did Periyar stop me or tell Kazhagam members?

Since the Salem conference, I have had no interest in elections. When a by-election occurred in Tiruchirappalli district some months ago and Comrade Rathinam of Karur wished to stand, I insisted upon and obtained his written resignation from the Kazhagam. Since Salem I have not participated in any election campaign meeting, though Periyar has spoken at a few.

It is therefore wrong to accuse the DMK of secretly seeking office and elections; even the accusers know it. Yet, poor people, what can they do? They must say something to disrupt our effort, so they try irrelevant and meaningless accusations. It is fruitless work. DMK comrades need not worry.

They say some friends and I entered public life only to earn our food. No abuse could be more degrading, yet I endure it. The public never saw me begging with bowl in hand, nor did they see me receive a golden bowl after joining Periyar. I know what their verdict will be.

Receiving such low language in public life is as ordinary as moonlight rice; it is no real poison. With my unfortunate ears I have heard thousands say Periyar spoke after taking money from Muslims, the British and Congress. Did that diminish my attachment or respect for him? I am abused similarly now. Why grieve? Analysis might even bring the petty pleasure of thinking, “Oh, perhaps our position has grown so strong that only slander of this kind can destroy us.”

I do not even seek that pleasure. I take pride in considering myself “of Periyar's lineage,” by which I mean the quality of enduring false accusation and degrading words.

Friends, they abuse you as they abuse me. Do not worry. Continue enthusiastically and skilfully establishing DMK branches everywhere. You will later see those who reviled you arrive to celebrate friendship.

They expected us to use the marriage issue as a pretext to seize the Dravidar Kazhagam, talk about that attempt, and give them a way to eliminate us. They were disappointed. We chose another road and created the DMK.

They expected attacks, contests and invasions. We said, “The path is new; I go my way, O Supreme,” leaving their sharpened sword useless and their assembled army without work. Bewildered, they decided to provoke a fight. Calling you innocents and me traitor belongs to that effort. But we are not bewildered; we are clear, and refuse to tremble at abuse.

They next looked for a change of principle in the new organisation as a pretext for conflict. They found none, because our principles have not changed.

They expected branches of the organisation founded by “these fellows” not to grow. From Madras to Tuticorin, branches grow beautifully everywhere. Naturally this brings disappointment and bewilderment. What can they do? They abuse us.

The wolf reportedly told the lamb, “Even if you committed no wrong, your mother did earlier, so I punish you now.” Their speech about us is stranger still: they condemn the DMK today for crimes it will supposedly commit in future.

One day a master angrily called his servant dog and demon. A friend, shocked because the servant had done nothing, asked why he was abusing him without cause. The master replied: “He has done nothing, but if he commits a fault someday, I may not then have time to rebuke him, so I do it now.”

The leader's supporter adopts the same perverse approach, arguing that the DMK will err in future and therefore must be condemned today. This reveals disappointment. Otherwise he would confidently wait and smash the DMK when it actually erred.

He cannot, because he knows the DMK will not err, deviate from principle or acquire qualities that ruin the Dravidian people. Unable to trust that a future error will provide an opportunity, he tries accusing it in advance.

The father of rationalism tries to wage war by a “puranic method.” The purana says that after a heavenly voice warned Kamsa that Devaki's son would destroy him, he believed he could escape by killing all her children. Likewise, Periyar sets out to oppose the DMK now because it might cause harm in future. Why should he try the wolf's method or Kamsa's method? I truly grieve at this condition.

His abuse of you as innocents and me as traitor is certainly the result of disappointment, and more disappointments will follow. Some hear him and think we work for selfish purposes; our work will clear their confusion. They will be disappointed.

They expect us to enter elections so they can attack us with ridicule and condemnation. They will be disappointed there too. More plainly, Periyar will turn the Dravidar Kazhagam into an election campaign instrument, and many present supporters will then come to the DMK camp.

They expect the DMK to rain pearls for thirty days and then sit in a corner, imagining their curse will work. They will be disappointed, because their predictions rest upon the fundamental error of considering you innocents and me a traitor. You are not innocents and I am not a traitor.

He imagines I incited you with treacherous thought and that you were deceived, then builds castle upon castle of argument upon it. What occurred was the reverse: the clear and courageous views you expressed formed the idea in my mind. He does not know it. Because his foundation is wrong, everything raised upon it cannot stand.

I have often grown weary of public life, but never wished to remain in it forever to build a golden fortress for myself.

I remember travelling more than ten years ago near Jolarpettai in a motor car with Periyar and Ponnambalanar. I said one should enter public life only after forty, having enjoyed family life until then, because afterward one could work intensely like him. Periyar patted my back and smilingly said: “You madman! Can one not enjoy life's happiness while remaining in public life? You do not know how.”

From that day to this, what tendency has he seen in me to use public life for myself? He had the right to rebuke me. Did an occasion ever arise?

His supporters now list my conduct in August, not wearing a black shirt, not going to Tuticorin, and writing the stories *Velaikkari* and *Nallathambi*, like pounding a mortar while thinking of beaten rice. Yet only after all those things did he hand me the “cash-box key” at Erode. How was that possible? This “traitor” received the key at the Erode conference. Did even one comrade then think of the “treason” spoken of today? Did Periyar? If so, would he have said, “I hand over the key”?

Can they imagine the public will forget all this?

“Traitor”—how lightly he makes the accusation!

“People who fatten their bellies through public life”—what a degrading charge. What evidence can be offered except that I am poor and he is the son of a millionaire?

Will the public weigh and calculate only my life after hearing it? Will they not consider Periyar's too? If carrying the palanquin brought so much reward, people will naturally say the rider must have gained even more. Such irresponsible speech discredits public life itself, not me.

Disappointment has placed Periyar in a state where he cannot consider these implications. He hopes abuse will provoke us into heated words he can use as a trump card. Here it becomes clear that after fifteen years he still has not understood me correctly.

I do not abuse those who abuse me. I do not know obstinacy and do not pursue useless work. Though reviled, labelled traitor and chased from public life, I do not leap about in anger. I ask only whether the principle and course I have adopted are right; I do not think, “They abuse us—shall we remain quiet?”

I am very ordinary and do not consider myself omnipotent. I always find satisfaction in saying, “At least this much was possible; at least we did this.” Today I see myself as your representative, carrying out your work with the resolve and enthusiasm arising from all your hearts as my fortress. I do not stand at the front through foolish thoughts of my own cleverness or power.

Periyar mistakenly imagines I waved a magic wand, assembled all these people and made them dance. That is profoundly wrong. Thousands of comrades firmly committed to principle ordered me to work and give me their support. They are not the innocents Periyar describes. Having long worked in Periyar's great army, how could they be? They are enthusiastic workers.

That is why DMK branches have already flourished in so many places.

Friends, continue working with the thought “We shall perform our duty skilfully” as your fortress. Arrows of abuse cannot pierce a resolute heart. I shall not disappear under the title “traitor,” nor will the insult “innocents” make you powerless.

He speaks so in anger. Before our mind's eye stands another Periyar—not in wedding attire, but the Periyar who regarded us as his children and affectionately called us jewels and gems. That image remains indelible. We created the DMK and work because a stain has settled upon the wealth of principle he gave us.

Only if we truly possessed treacherous thought, selfishness or the baseness to use the Kazhagam as a tool would his accusations make us angry. He imagines in us what is absent; such has become his condition.

If a man with defective sight glimpses sandalwood paste upon a body and cries, “Alas, this man has leprosy!”, would one become angry? One would only laugh.

*[The available source ends here without a closing paragraph or publication date.]*
